# NestJS file structure

Follow NestJS docs and `nest g`: one main concept per file, named `<name>.<kind>.ts`.

## One concept per file

- One class, or one cohesive group of constants + types + helpers for one idea, per file.
- Split when a file mixes unrelated concepts or grows past ~200 lines.
- Small private helpers used only by that concept stay in its file.

## Names and folders

| Kind | File | Folder |
|---|---|---|
| module / controller / service | `attendance.module.ts`, `attendance.controller.ts`, `attendance.service.ts` | feature folder `modules/<feature>/` |
| DTO | `create-check-in.dto.ts` | `<feature>/dto/` |
| interface / type-only file | `api-response.interface.ts` | `<feature>/interfaces/` |
| exception | `face-not-matched.exception.ts` | `<feature>/exceptions/` |
| decorator | `roles.decorator.ts` | `<feature>/decorators/` |
| guard, interceptor, filter, pipe, middleware | `roles.guard.ts`, `response.interceptor.ts`, ... | `guards/`, `interceptors/`, `filters/`, `pipes/`, `middleware/` |
| config | `redis.config.ts` | `src/config/` |
| queue processors | `attendance-log.processor.ts` | `<feature>/` or `processors/` |

Code shared by several features goes in `src/common/<area>/` with the same subfolders.

## No barrel files

Never add `index.ts` files that re-export a folder; they cause circular imports between Nest modules. Import from the exact file (ESM: with `.js`):

```typescript
// BAD
import { MatchResult } from '../vision/index.js';
// GOOD
import { MatchResult } from '../vision/match-result.js';
```

## Tests next to the code

- Unit tests beside the file: `attendance.service.ts` → `attendance.service.spec.ts`.
- Only e2e tests (`*.e2e-spec.ts`) live in `test/`.
